 "findings": [
  "At real length, compaction is the dominant cost lever: `none` (FIFO truncation) costs 5-50x more than any folding policy, because dropping the oldest turn changes the front of the prompt and re-prefills everything behind it.",
  "`pins` is 1.9-2.7x cheaper than `compact` at equal prompt size, and the entire difference is the summary call: 13 folds x ~92k pre-fold prompt = 1.22M of compact's 2.03M cost in the 871-turn session.",
  "Masking alone is not a cost win: it is sometimes worse than `none` (127k vs 70k; 293k vs 100k) because it changes the prefix without reducing the prompt enough to avoid FIFO.",
  "The toy-regime 6.38% drop saving was an artifact of L0=1,515; at L0=38,408 the journal is most of the prompt and dropping it is the whole game."
 ],
 "caveats": [
  "The model assumes a perfectly stable prefix, so absolute cache-hit rates (0.99 for folding policies) are optimistic; the real 871-turn session reported 0.753.",
  "The ranking is robust because the summary-call count is a direct count, not a cache-model output.",
  "The quality half of the claim (pins retain constraints a summary loses) is NOT measured here; that is what E7's graded arms exist to test."
 ]
}
